.lua
book.chapter(31, "A complete expression-language toolchain")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Define a small language completely"
.pp
We now build a complete toolchain for a prefix arithmetic language. Inputs
are integer atoms or parenthesized add and mul forms with exactly two operands.
The tool parses text, validates structure and numeric range, lowers to an
expression ADT, evaluates, folds constants, and emits canonical source.
Prefix syntax keeps the example focused on representation boundaries rather
than operator precedence. Nothing is delegated to Frontend.eval: our language
has its own explicitly implemented semantics.
.lua
book.grammar("The case-study input language", {
    {"expression", 'integer | "(add" expression expression ")"'},
    {"expression", '"(mul" expression expression ")"'}
})
book.chain({"Source bytes", "SExpr syntax", "Validated Expr", "Folded Expr", "Canonical source"},
    "The case study establishes an invariant at each boundary")
.endlua
.pp
Arithmetic uses MetaML's wrapping Int operations. There are no variables,
effects, division, or user-defined functions in the input language. These
restrictions matter to optimization: evaluating both children and folding
their arithmetic preserves the specified semantics. If effects or exceptions
were later introduced, apparently simple algebraic rewrites could change
observable behavior. An optimizer's correctness argument always depends on
the language it transforms.
.sh 2 "Parse flexible syntax, then validate a stronger model"
.pp
SExpr.parse_one handles balanced structure and atom boundaries. It can accept
many expressions outside our language, so a lowering function checks tags,
arity, and numeric conversion. The Expr ADT then guarantees that every Add
and Mul has exactly two expression children and every Lit contains a valid
Int. Later semantic passes need not repeatedly check arbitrary child lists.
This division makes failures precise and successful passes simpler.
.pp
The following listing is self-contained. Result.map2 combines the two
recursive lowering results. Its arguments are eager, so both operands are
lowered before map2 chooses a result; when both fail, the first error is
preferred. That is an intentional bounded example policy, not a claim that
the compiler stops all work immediately after the first malformed child.
A larger implementation could use and_then for strict short-circuiting.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[type Expr := | Lit Int | Add Expr Expr | Mul Expr Expr

lower syntax := match syntax with
  | Atom text -> Int.from_string text |> Result.from_option "invalid integer"
      |> Result.map Lit
  | SList [Atom "add", a, b] -> Result.map2 Add (lower a) (lower b)
  | SList [Atom "mul", a, b] -> Result.map2 Mul (lower a) (lower b)
  | _ -> Err "expected an integer or a binary add/mul form"

parse source := SExpr.parse_one source |> Result.and_then lower

evaluate expr := match expr with
  | Lit n -> n
  | Add a b -> evaluate a + evaluate b
  | Mul a b -> evaluate a * evaluate b

fold expr := match expr with
  | Lit n -> Lit n
  | Add a b ->
      let left := fold a in
      let right := fold b in
      (match (left, right) with
        | (Lit x, Lit y) -> Lit (x + y)
        | _ -> Add left right)
  | Mul a b ->
      let left := fold a in
      let right := fold b in
      (match (left, right) with
        | (Lit x, Lit y) -> Lit (x * y)
        | _ -> Mul left right)

emit expr := match expr with
  | Lit n -> Int.to_string n
  | Add a b -> "(add " ^ emit a ^ " " ^ emit b ^ ")"
  | Mul a b -> "(mul " ^ emit a ^ " " ^ emit b ^ ")"

compile source := parse source |> Result.map (fold >> emit)
source := "(add 2 (mul 3 4))"
tree := Result.unwrap (parse source)
Test.equal "interpret" (evaluate tree) 14
Test.equal "compile" (compile source) (Ok "14")
Test.equal "preserve semantics" (evaluate (fold tree)) (evaluate tree)
Test.equal "round trip" (parse (emit tree)) (Ok tree)
Test.equal "wrong arity" (compile "(add 1)")
  (Err "expected an integer or a binary add/mul form")
Test.equal "unknown operation" (compile "(sub 3 2)")
  (Err "expected an integer or a binary add/mul form")
Test.equal "bad integer" (compile "(add x 1)") (Err "invalid integer")
Test.check "unbalanced source" (Result.is_err (compile "(add 1 2"))
samples := ["0", "(add 1 2)", "(mul (add 1 2) (add 3 4))"]
Test.check "semantic preservation over corpus"
  (List.all (fn text ->
    let expr := Result.unwrap (parse text) in
    evaluate (fold expr) == evaluate expr) samples)]=],
    "A complete parser, validator, evaluator, optimizer, and emitter")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Explain the optimization"
.pp
Fold transforms children before their parent. Once both child results are
literals, it evaluates the corresponding operation and constructs one Lit.
Because this language has only closed arithmetic expressions, every valid
finite input eventually becomes a literal. The fallback branches preserve
structure and make the intended extension point visible if variables are
added later. They are not evidence that variables already exist in this
version of the language.
.pp
The preservation argument is structural induction. A literal's value is
unchanged. Assuming folding preserves each child, replacing their Add or
Mul with the same wrapping operation on their values preserves the parent.
Thus the entire tree's value is preserved. This argument does not rely on
floating-point associativity or an unbounded integer model. Keeping the
arithmetic contract explicit makes the proof match the executable program.
.sh 2 "Test relationships between stages"
.pp
The tests cover more than a final answer. Parsing emitted syntax reconstructs
the original domain tree. Evaluating a folded tree equals evaluating its
input. Compilation rejects malformed arities, unknown operators, bad numeric
atoms, and unbalanced input. These properties exercise different boundaries:
syntax parsing, semantic lowering, transformation, and output reconstruction.
A single successful arithmetic example could not establish all of them.
.pp
Canonical emission uses decimal integer text and explicit prefix delimiters.
It intentionally discards whitespace and the original integer spelling.
The round-trip contract is tree equality, not byte equality with the user's
source. A source editor would need to preserve trivia and spans separately.
The current error strings likewise identify a category but not a precise
source range; adding annotated syntax is the next step for an editor-quality
diagnostic experience.
.sh 2 "Extend one contract at a time"
.pp
Adding variables requires a Var constructor, an environment, unknown-name
errors, and a revised evaluator result type. Constant folding would then
retain nonconstant subtrees. Adding division requires explicit zero and
overflow behavior. Adding effects invalidates rewrites that erase or duplicate
operands. Adding infix syntax changes the frontend while the validated ADT
can remain stable. These are separate changes, each with its own tests.
.pp
For production use, bound input size and nesting and choose an execution
budget appropriate to the application. This example uses ordinary recursive
tree functions, so very deep input consumes host resources. A tail-recursive
worklist can replace some traversals, but output size and retained tree memory
remain real costs. Resource behavior belongs in a toolchain's contract
alongside accepted syntax and semantic correctness.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
The implementation combines SExpr, Result, List, and the language's own ADTs
and recursion. Compare it with the generic AST visitor chapter and the native
rewriting facilities later in the book. Extend the compiler with variables,
then test that folding preserves evaluation under several environments.
Retain the existing malformed-input tests so extending the language does
not accidentally weaken the validation boundary that subsequent passes rely on.
